  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 306 AM MDT Tue Jul 21 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nMonsoon moisture continues to stream eastward on the northern \nperiphery of a strengthening ridge of high pressure centered over \nthe Great Basin. While the moisture aloft thickens from top to \nbottom, variable mid- and higher level clouds and a few spotty \nlight showers or virga will be the main impact this morning. \nWidely scattered showers and thunderstorms will begin creeping \nback northward into the southwest this afternoon and evening; \notherwise, mostly dry and warm conditions will prevail elsewhere. \n\nA subtle shortwave will move through the ridge on Wednesday and \nwill provide sufficient forcing for a more concentrated area of \nshowers and embedded thunderstorms over southwest and portions of \ncentral Montana. While ML CAPE values look to be around or less \nthan 500 J/kg, the combination of forcing and PWATS well over an \ninch will increase instances of excessive downpours capable of \nlocalized flooding, particularly for recent burn scars, urban \nareas, and other flood prone locales. Higher CAPE values around \n500 to 800 J/kg look to be present on the northern fringe of this \narea of precipitation, mainly for areas along the central MT \nhighway 87/200 corridor southward to I90. These areas will see \nspottier rainfall, but higher occurrences of strong, to severe \nstorms with strong, gusty winds, hail, and downpours in addition \nto cloud to ground lightning. \n\nThe exiting shortwave will push the richest moisture to the east \nand leave a drier boundary layer in its wake on Thursday; however,\nanother shortwave moves in from the southwest and brings more \nscattered showers and storms to much of the forecast area. \nForecast soundings show inverted V soundings, a key indicator of \nstronger thunderstorm outflow winds. Hail, downpours, and cloud to\nground lightning will also be concerns with Thursday's storms. \nThe combination of the drying conditions and the building ridge \nwill also warm afternoon temperatures well into the 90s and even \nsome 100s for most lower elevation locations. \n\nThe moisture profile will continue to thin out from west to east \non Friday, but there may be enough lingering instability and \nmoisture for some showers and storms over the eastern portions of \nthe forecast area. Otherwise, strengthening dry southwesterly flow\nwill increase surface winds for at least the first half of the \nweekend while temperatures remain hot. This may introduce elevated\nfire weather concerns if the mid- week rains are too light or \nscattered in nature. - RCG \n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nEnsemble guidance has been persistent in narrowing down the \nheavier rain threat to southwestern areas south of I90. NBM \nprobabilistic guidance has increased the chances for a half inch \nof rain for the 24 hour period ending 6 am Thursday up to around \n30% for lower elevations and 60 to 80% over higher terrain. This \nis significant because these probabilities typically do not \nreflect the localized heavier rainfall from thunderstorms. The \nmain uncertainty is the lower CAPE and whether these rainfall \namounts will fall in a timeframe less than six hours in order to \nproduce higher localized flash flooding of burn scars, urban \ncenters, and other flood prone areas. \n\nRainfall becomes much more scattered in nature for central and \nsouthwestern locations north of I90, but higher CAPE and moisture \nlevels may result in much higher rainfall rates in short periods \nof time for more impactful flooding, albeit in a highly localized \nfashion. The Weather Prediction Center has recently raised the \nflood risk to the 'slight' level (5 to 15% chance) for areas south\nof I90 while maintaining a 'marginal' risk level for central and \nsouthwestern areas north of I90 and south of the central MT \nhighway 87/200 corridor. This situation will continue to be \nmonitored for potential flood products over the next 24 hours. \n- RCG",
